% ── Abstract ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\begin{abstract}
We present \textsc{CppSim}, a C++/HIP 2D Ising simulator for
Heisenberg-picture tensor network time evolution on AMD GPUs.
Using float32 throughout instead of the ComplexF32 representation
of the Julia reference, \textsc{CppSim} halves the memory footprint
per element, enabling $\chimax = 120$ on a single 32\,GB GPU where
the reference tops out at $\chimax \approx 75$, while achieving
twice the throughput at all bond dimensions.
The key contributions are: First, a zero-malloc GPU workspace that
pre-allocates all buffers at startup; Second, a custom HIP tensor
permutation kernel replacing host-side index shuffling with a pure
device-to-device operation, yielding a 7.6$\times$ trotter speedup;
Third, a hybrid QR strategy selecting Cholesky-QR for tall-skinny
matrices and Householder-QR otherwise; Fourth, adaptive Belief
Propagation with log-space Bethe partition function evaluation and
explicit sign tracking.
We validate correctness against golden-value tests and demonstrate
$\chi$-convergence of the spin autocorrelation function $C(t)$ for
$\chi \in \{50, 70, 80, 100\}$ on a $4\times4$ grid.
An analytic memory model, validated to within 2\% against hardware
measurements, projects feasibility for a $10\times10$ grid at
$\chimax=100$ on AMD MI350 hardware ($\approx109$\,GB HBM).
Performance across AMD GPU generations (MI60~GFX906, MI100~GFX908)
reveals that at saturation the workload is memory-bandwidth-bound,
and that architecture-specific GEMM kernel paths affect both timing
and numerical reproducibility at large $\chi$.
\end{abstract}

