\subsection{BP Convergence Anomaly}
\label{sec:bp_convergence}

At $\chi=100$, layer $t=19$ produces $C(19) = 0.009$ instead of the expected
$\approx 0.057$; layer $t=20$ immediately recovers to $0.054$. The per-layer
timing (Table~\ref{tab:breakdown}) shows that BP is adaptive: layers $t=17$ and
$t=20$ are genuinely hard, requiring $\approx 672$\,s of BP, while layers
$t=18$ and $t=19$ converge in $\approx 154$\,s and $\approx 161$\,s respectively.
The anomalous layer $t=19$ does not stand out in BP time relative to layer $t=18$,
so the anomaly is not caused by early BP termination.
The Trotter timing is identical across all saturated layers, ruling out a
computation error in the gate update itself.
The most likely explanation is a transient sign error in the Bethe partition
function~\eqref{eq:bethe}: the product of $\mathrm{sign}(Z_e)$ terms can flip
due to a marginally negative $Z_e$ at an unlucky BP fixed point, producing a
sign change in $C(t)$ that corrects itself once the next Trotter layer resets
the message landscape. Diagnosing this definitively would require logging the
per-bond $Z_e$ values and sweep counts at $t=19$, which is left for future work.

