\subsection{rocBLAS Tensile Coverage}

rocBLAS generates optimized GEMM kernels via the Tensile library for specific
(architecture, matrix-dimension) pairs. On MI60 (GFX906), Tensile lacks
pre-built kernels for the GEMM shapes arising at $\chi = 120$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item QR GEMM: $m \times n = 1{,}728{,}000 \times 480$
  \item SVD input: $k_{1D} \times k_{2D} = 1{,}920 \times 1{,}920$
\end{itemize}
At $\chi=100$, MI60 runs correctly; the practical ceiling is approximately $\chi \approx 110$.
Different ROCm Docker images are required per architecture family (GFX906 vs.\ GFX908).

\paragraph{Numerical precision across architectures.}
A subtler portability issue is observed in the $\chi$-convergence of $C(t)$:
at $\chimax=100$, MI60 produces values that drift systematically below MI100
by $1$--$3\times10^{-3}$ at late times ($t \geq 12$), with the deviation growing
monotonically across layers.
This drift is absent at $\chimax \leq 80$ and is not caused by sign anomalies
in the Bethe partition function (which appear at all $\chi$ values on both GPUs
and recover within one layer).
The most likely source is the gram matrix GEMM in the Cholesky-QR path
(\S\ref{sec:qr}): $\mathbf{C} = \mathbf{cv}^\top \mathbf{cv}$ is a float32
reduction over $k = \chimax^3$ terms ($k = 10^6$ at $\chi=100$).
GFX906 (Vega20) implements SGEMM via scalar vector-ALU instructions (no
native matrix multiply units), while GFX908 (CDNA1) uses dedicated MFMA
instructions with a different reduction tree.
Floating-point addition is non-associative, so the two reduction orders
yield different rounding errors; the effect grows with $k = \chi^3$, consistent
with the observed $\chi$-dependence of the drift.
The Julia reference uses the same Cholesky-QR algorithm but in float64
(\texttt{\_chol} variant), which makes the gram matrix computation essentially
exact regardless of summation order.

Two experiments would isolate this mechanism.
First, replacing the float32 gram matrix GEMM with a mixed-precision call
(\texttt{rocblas\_gemm\_ex} with \texttt{compute\_type = f64}) while keeping
all other operations in float32 would confirm whether the gram matrix is the
source: if MI60 and MI100 agree afterward, the reduction order in that single
GEMM is responsible.
Second, a complete float64 implementation at $\chimax=50$ would provide an
architecture-independent reference and enable direct comparison with the Julia
baseline.
Since the workload at saturation is memory-bandwidth-bound (Section~\ref{sec:performance},
Fig.~\ref{fig:efficiency}), moving to float64 doubles the data traffic but does
not increase the compute bottleneck; on both MI60 and MI100 the timing penalty
would be modest and the accuracy gain substantial.

The analytic memory model (\S\ref{sec:memory_model}) extends these observations to a
range of current and future GPUs. Table~\ref{tab:max_chi} gives the memory-limited
$\chimax$ for square grids up to $10\times10$, showing that AMD MI300X (192\,GB) and
NVIDIA H100 (80\,GB) reach similar $\chimax$ for the grids of interest, while the
64\,GB-per-die MI250X sits between them.

