\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
% ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

We have presented \textsc{CppSim}, a C++/HIP implementation of Heisenberg-picture
tensor network dynamics for the 2D Ising model, targeting AMD GPU hardware.
The key contributions are:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item A \textbf{zero-malloc GPU workspace} making peak VRAM known exactly before
        the first Trotter layer and eliminating all allocator overhead.
  \item A \textbf{GPU tensor permutation kernel} yielding a 7.6$\times$ speedup
        over CPU-side index shuffling, the dominant bottleneck in prior work.
  \item An \textbf{analytic memory model} validated against hardware measurements
        to within 2\%, enabling GPU deployment planning across generations.
  \item A \textbf{sign-correct Bethe partition function} resolving late-time
        $C(t)$ anomalies present in simpler implementations.
  \item A \textbf{chi convergence study} establishing $\chi \approx 70$ as the
        minimum for accurate late-time dynamics on $4\times4$ at these parameters,
        with $C(t=20)$ converging to $\approx 0.053$ as $\chi \to 100$.
\end{enumerate}

The simulator achieves 3$\times$ speedup over the Julia reference at $\chi=50$
and runs bond dimensions up to $\chi=120$ on a single 32\,GB AMD GPU.
The validated memory model projects feasibility for the primary research target:
$10\times10$ at $\chi=100$ on AMD MI350 ($\approx 109$\,GB HBM),
opening a new scale of 2D quantum dynamics simulation on AMD hardware.

